      {
        "id": "lem-rpm-bmt-transport-algebra",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "BMT transport constraint and restricted-ansatz algebra",
        "reference": "items/lem-rpm-bmt-transport-algebra.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-cross-product-in-r3",
          "def-gr-metric-selected-connection",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
          "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative",
          "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-metric-selected-connection": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice for the canonical tangent-bundle smooth structure supplied by the GR items below. Let $(M,g,N)$ be a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold with its metric-compatible connection $\\nabla$, let $c>0$, and let $z:I\\to M$ be a $C^2$ future timelike curve parametrized by proper time $\\tau$, with $U=D_\\tau z$ and $g(U,U)=-c^2$. Let $F_\\tau$ be a continuous alternating two-form along $z$. Define $F^\\sharp_\\tau$ by\n$$g(F^\\sharp_\\tau(v),w)=F_\\tau(w,v).$$\nThen $F^\\sharp_\\tau$ is skew-adjoint with respect to $g$.\n\nFor constants $\\alpha,a,b\\in\\mathbb R$, consider the restricted linear transport equations\n$$D_\\tau U=\\alpha F^\\sharp(U),\\qquad D_\\tau s=\\alpha\\left(aF^\\sharp(s)+\\frac{b}{c^2}U\\,\\kappa\\right),\\qquad \\kappa:=g(s,F^\\sharp(U)).$$\nFor every initial $s(\\tau_0)$ there is a unique solution on each compact subinterval of $I$. Along any such solution,\n$$\\frac{d}{d\\tau}g(U,s)=\\alpha(1-a-b)\\kappa.$$\nConsequently, the orthogonality constraint $g(U,s)=0$ is preserved when $a+b=1$, and on that constraint $g(s,s)$ is constant. For $\\alpha\\ne0$, matching the rest-frame spatial law\n$$\\frac{d\\mathbf s}{d\\tau}=\\alpha G\\,(\\mathbf s\\times\\mathbf B)$$\nfor all spatial $\\mathbf s,\\mathbf B$, with $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, forces $a=G$ and $b=1-G$. This uniqueness is only within the displayed two-term ansatz; no uniqueness among all covariant spin-transport laws is asserted. If $\\alpha=0$, the rest-frame equation does not identify $a$ or $b$.",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": [
          {
            "title": "University of Virginia, Relativistic Spin Precession: the BMT Equation, Electromagnetic Theory lecture",
            "url": "https://galileoandeinstein.phys.virginia.edu/Elec_Mag/2022_Lectures/EM_71_g-2.html",
            "locator": "Section “Relativistic Spin Precession: the BMT Equation”, four-spin construction and coefficient determination; the local statement below is proved independently."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-rpm-body-spin-size-bound",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "Positive-energy spin–size inequality",
        "reference": "items/lem-rpm-body-spin-size-bound.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-cross-product-in-r3",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
          "def-l-one-of-a-measure",
          "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
          "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm",
          "thm-integral-triangle-inequality",
          "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one",
          "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-l-one-of-a-measure": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
          "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-integral-triangle-inequality": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "statement": "Fix $c>0$ and a future unit observer $n$ in an affine Minkowski spacetime as in [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]]. Identify the positive-definite rest space $n^\\perp$ with $\\mathbb R^3$ and its Euclidean measure $d^3x$. Let $e:\\mathbb R^3\\to[0,\\infty)$ and $j:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be Lebesgue-integrable, both zero almost everywhere outside $\\overline B_\\ell(0)$ for some $\\ell>0$. Suppose $|j(x)|\\le e(x)/c$ almost everywhere. Define\n$$E_0:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e(x)\\,d^3x>0,\\qquad M:=E_0/c^2,\\qquad \\mathbf s_*:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}x\\times j(x)\\,d^3x.$$\nThen $\\mathbf s_*$ is absolutely integrable and\n$$|\\mathbf s_*|\\le\\ell Mc,\\qquad\\text{equivalently}\\qquad \\ell\\ge\\frac{|\\mathbf s_*|}{Mc}.$$\nThe hypotheses are the full mathematical content of this estimate; it is not a bound for a quantum point particle or for a source whose energy density or momentum density fails them.",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": [
          {
            "title": "L. Filipe Costa and José Natário, Center of mass, spin supplementary conditions, and the momentum of spinning particles, arXiv:1410.6443v2; published in Equations of Motion in Relativistic Gravity, Fundamental Theories of Physics 179 (Springer, 2015)",
            "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1410.6443v2",
            "locator": "Section 2, dominant-energy-condition bound on the Møller radius; this item proves the stated integral inequality under its explicit pointwise hypotheses and restores SI units."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-rpm-charge-multipole-remainder",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "Finite-size charge multipole remainder",
        "reference": "items/lem-rpm-charge-multipole-remainder.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
          "def-l-one-of-a-measure",
          "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
          "thm-extreme-value-metric",
          "thm-ftc-second-part",
          "thm-integral-triangle-inequality",
          "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-l-one-of-a-measure": "mathematical-premise",
          "prop-order-and-scalar-rules-for-the-nonnegative-integral": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-extreme-value-metric": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-ftc-second-part": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-integral-triangle-inequality": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "statement": "Let $\\ell>0$, $z\\in\\mathbb R^3$, and let $\\rho\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ be real and zero almost everywhere outside $\\overline B_\\ell(z)$. Write $\\|\\rho\\|_1:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|\\rho(y)|\\,d^3y$. Let $\\mathcal U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open with $\\overline B_\\ell(z)\\subset\\mathcal U$, and let $b:\\mathcal U\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be $C^2$ for a finite integer $m\\ge1$. For each component $b_k$, let\n$$M_k:=\\sup_{x\\in\\overline B_\\ell(z)}\\|D^2b_k(x)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}<\\infty,$$\nwhere the operator norm is taken on the bilinear map using the Euclidean norm in [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Define $Q:=\\int\\rho\\,d^3y$, $d:=\\int(y-z)\\rho(y)\\,d^3y$, and $\\mathcal B:=\\int\\rho(y)b(y)\\,d^3y$, with vector integrals taken componentwise. Then\n$$\\mathcal B_k=Q\\,b_k(z)+Db_k(z)d+R_k,\\qquad |R_k|\\le\\frac12M_k\\ell^2\\|\\rho\\|_1\\quad(k=1,\\ldots,m).$$\nThus every fixed component in a smooth finite-component trivialization has the stated charge multipole remainder. A tensor-valued component may be treated only after a fixed smooth trivialization over the ball; derivatives of that trivialization enter $M_k$, and no covariant tensor remainder without this data is asserted.",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": [
          {
            "title": "Carnegie Mellon 21-269, Riemann integration notes",
            "url": "https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-269/notes/rint.html",
            "locator": "Fundamental-theorem-of-calculus method used for the integral Taylor remainder; the item derives the bound directly."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-rpm-flat-centroid-shift",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "Exact flat centroid translation and disk",
        "reference": "items/lem-rpm-flat-centroid-shift.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-cross-product-in-r3",
          "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
          "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-cross-product-in-r3": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "statement": "Let $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be an oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski space, with signature $(-+++)$, and fix $c>0$. Let $p$ be a future timelike vector with rest-frame components $p=(Mc,\\mathbf 0)$ for $M>0$. Let $S^{ab}=-S^{ba}$ satisfy the momentum supplementary condition $S^{ab}p_b=0$, and set $s_*^k:=\\tfrac12\\epsilon_{kij}S^{ij}$ in an oriented orthonormal rest-space basis. For every future unit observer\n$$n=\\gamma(1,\\mathbf v/c),\\qquad \\gamma=(1-|\\mathbf v|^2/c^2)^{-1/2},\\qquad |\\mathbf v|<c,$$\ndefine\n$$\\delta^a:=\\frac{S^{ab}n_b}{p\\cdot n},\\qquad S'^{ab}:=S^{ab}-(\\delta^ap^b-\\delta^bp^a).$$\nThen $p\\cdot n\\ne0$, $\\delta\\cdot p=\\delta\\cdot n=0$, and $S'^{ab}n_b=0$. If the representative point changes by $z\\mapsto z+\\delta$, the total angular momentum $J=z\\wedge p+S$ is unchanged by this stipulated spin-shift rule. In the rest space,\n$$\\boldsymbol\\delta=\\frac{\\mathbf s_*\\times\\mathbf v}{Mc^2},$$\nIf $\\mathbf s_*\\ne0$, the shifts of all future timelike observers fill the open disk in $\\mathbf s_*^\\perp$ of radius $|\\mathbf s_*|/(Mc)$; the boundary is a supremum and is not attained. If $\\mathbf s_*=0$, only the zero shift occurs. This is an exact affine-flat algebraic result.",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": [
          {
            "title": "L. Filipe Costa and José Natário, Center of mass, spin supplementary conditions, and the momentum of spinning particles, arXiv:1410.6443v2; published in Equations of Motion in Relativistic Gravity, Fundamental Theories of Physics 179 (Springer, 2015)",
            "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1410.6443v2",
            "locator": "Section 2, observer-dependent centroid shift, the centroid disk and the Møller radius; the result below restores SI units and treats timelike observers only."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-rpm-ssc-momentum-identity",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "Differentiated SSC momentum–velocity identity",
        "reference": "items/lem-rpm-ssc-momentum-identity.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-covariant-derivative-along-a-curve",
          "def-gr-metric-selected-connection",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
          "def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time",
          "lem-gr-observer-pairing-with-future-causal-vectors",
          "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-covariant-derivative-along-a-curve": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-metric-selected-connection": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-gr-observer-pairing-with-future-causal-vectors": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice as inherited by the supplied Lorentzian smooth-structure and connection results. Let $z:I\\to M$ be a $C^2$ future timelike worldline in a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,N)$, parametrized by proper time, with $U=D_\\tau z$ and $g(U,U)=-c^2$. Let $p$ be a $C^1$ vector field along $z$, let $S^{ab}=-S^{ba}$ and $\\mathcal T^{ab}=-\\mathcal T^{ba}$ be $C^1$ bivector fields along $z$, and let $n$ be a $C^1$ future unit timelike field along $z$. Suppose\n$$S^{ab}n_b=0,\\qquad D_\\tau S^{ab}=p^aU^b-p^bU^a+\\mathcal T^{ab}.$$\nDefine $\\gamma:=-g(n,U)/c$ and $E_n:=-c\\,g(p,n)$. Then $\\gamma>0$, and\n$$c\\gamma\\,p^a=\\frac{E_n}{c}U^a+S^{ab}D_\\tau n_b+\\mathcal T^{ab}n_b.$$\nHere $\\mathcal T$ denotes an arbitrary antisymmetric torque; setting it to zero gives the torque-free case.",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": [
          {
            "title": "L. Filipe Costa and José Natário, Center of mass, spin supplementary conditions, and the momentum of spinning particles, arXiv:1410.6443v2; published in Equations of Motion in Relativistic Gravity, Fundamental Theories of Physics 179 (Springer, 2015)",
            "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1410.6443v2",
            "locator": "Section 3, general momentum–velocity identity, with the sign and SI conventions stated locally here."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-rpm-td-local-closure",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "title": "Admissible TD pole-dipole local closure",
        "reference": "items/lem-rpm-td-local-closure.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-covariant-derivative-along-a-curve",
          "def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors",
          "def-gr-metric-selected-connection",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
          "def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time",
          "lem-gr-observer-pairing-with-future-causal-vectors",
          "lem-rpm-ssc-momentum-identity",
          "thm-gr-curvature-algebraic-symmetries",
          "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative",
          "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-covariant-derivative-along-a-curve": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-metric-selected-connection": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-gr-timelike-worldline-and-proper-time": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-gr-observer-pairing-with-future-causal-vectors": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-rpm-ssc-momentum-identity": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-gr-curvature-algebraic-symmetries": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and use a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,N)$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$ with its metric-compatible Levi–Civita connection. Use SI units: coordinates are metres, proper time is seconds, $c>0$ is in metres per second, $p$ is in kilogram metres per second, $S$ is in kilogram metres squared per second, and $R$ has inverse-square-metre components. Let $z:I\\to M$ be a $C^2$ future timelike worldline parametrized by proper time, with $U=dz/d\\tau$ and $g(U,U)=-c^2$. Let $p$ be a $C^1$ future timelike vector field along $z$, let $S^{ab}=-S^{ba}$ be a $C^1$ bivector field, and suppose the displayed evolution equations hold:\n\n$$D_\\tau p^a=f^a:=-\\frac12R^a{}_{bcd}U^bS^{cd},\\qquad D_\\tau S^{ab}=p^aU^b-p^bU^a.\\tag{1}$$\n\nPut $\\mu:=\\sqrt{-g(p,p)}>0$, $m:=-g(p,U)/c^2>0$, $C^a:=S^{ab}p_b$, and define the linear map\n\n$$K^a{}_b:=\\mu^2\\delta^a_b-\\frac12S^{ac}R_{cbde}S^{de}.$$\n\nAt every state where $K$ is invertible and $W:=K^{-1}p$ is future timelike, the TD condition $C=0$ and (1) imply\n\n$$KU=mc^2p,\\qquad U=\\frac{cW}{\\sqrt{-g(W,W)}}.\\tag{2}$$\n\nConversely, on the open state region where $p$ and $W$ are future timelike and $K$ is invertible, the normalized formula for $U$ in (2), together with (1), defines a smooth local ODE. A solution initialized with $C=0$ preserves $C=0$. While it remains in that admissible region, $\\mu$ and $S_{ab}S^{ab}/2$ are constant. If $S=0$, then $p=(\\mu/c)U$, $D_\\tau p=0$, and $D_\\tau U=0$. These are local statements; no continuation past loss of invertibility of $K$ or timelikeness of $W$ is asserted. Equations (1) are hypotheses of this mathematical lemma, not a physical derivation of a stress-energy moment model.",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": [
          {
            "title": "L. Filipe Costa and José Natário, Center of mass, spin supplementary conditions, and the momentum of spinning particles, arXiv:1410.6443v2; published in Equations of Motion in Relativistic Gravity, Fundamental Theories of Physics 179 (Springer, 2015)",
            "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1410.6443v2",
            "locator": "Section 3, equations (14)–(16), for the TD supplementary condition and pole-dipole momentum–velocity relation. The admissible open domain, local vector field, constraint preservation and continuation limits are proved here."
          },
          {
            "title": "Gerald Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems, American Mathematical Society, Graduate Studies in Mathematics 140 (2012)",
            "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-ode/ode.pdf",
            "locator": "Complete author-hosted book, Chapter 2, Theorem 2.2, for local existence and uniqueness of a locally Lipschitz coordinate vector field."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "def-rpm-body-moments",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "library": "physics",
        "title": "Body moments and transport convention",
        "reference": "items/def-rpm-body-moments.md#Definition",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-rpm-charge-spin-model",
          "lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed",
          "thm-gr-convex-normal-neighborhoods",
          "thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map",
          "thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-rpm-charge-spin-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-gr-convex-normal-neighborhoods": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sr-stress-balance-and-finite-flux": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "statement": "Use the charged/spinning body-model conventions of the now-authored batch-24 carrier [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]]: a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-+++)$, coordinates $x^0=ct$ in metres, $c>0$, and a $C^2$ future timelike representative curve $z:I\\to M$ parametrized by proper time $\\tau$ in seconds, with $U=dz/d\\tau$ and $g(U,U)=-c^2$. The actual supplier defines these quantities and the SI electromagnetic-tensor convention used here. Assume Countable Choice for the GR smooth-structure, convex-normal and parallel-transport suppliers used below.\n\n**(a) Flat isolated-body moments.** In an inertial chart of affine Minkowski space, let $T^{ab}$ be a smooth symmetric stress-energy tensor with components in $\\mathrm J\\,\\mathrm m^{-3}$ and spatially compact support on every finite time slab. At a fixed time $t$, define\n$$p^a(t):=\\frac1c\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}T^{0a}(ct,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3x,$$\nand, for a representative point $z(t)$ on that time slice, define\n$$S_z^{ab}(t):=\\frac1c\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\left((x-z(t))^aT^{0b}(ct,\\mathbf x)-(x-z(t))^bT^{0a}(ct,\\mathbf x)\\right)d^3x.$$\nHere $(x-z(t))^0=0$ on the chosen slice, $p$ has units $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, and $S_z$ has units $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-1}}$. These are definitions of moments; no conservation is asserted by clause (a).\n\n**(b) Local curved-body moments.** Let $z\\in M$ and let $U_0$ be a convex normal neighborhood containing $z$. Let $\\Sigma\\subset U_0$ be a spacelike $C^1$ hypersurface through $z$, with future unit normal $n_x$ and induced volume element $d\\Sigma$. Let a smooth symmetric stress-energy tensor $T^{ab}$ have support on $\\Sigma$ compactly contained in $U_0$, with all displayed integrals finite. For each support point $x\\in\\Sigma$, let $v(x):=\\exp_z^{-1}(x)\\in T_zM$ be the unique joining position vector supplied by [[thm-gr-convex-normal-neighborhoods]] and [[thm-gr-local-geodesics-and-exponential-map]]. Let $H_{x\\to z}:T_xM\\to T_zM$ be parallel transport along that unique geodesic, with existence, uniqueness and metric preservation as in [[lem-gr-parallel-transport-and-geodesic-speed]]. Define $T^\\sharp(n_x)^a:=T^{ab}(x)(n_x)_b$ and\n$$p_z:=\\frac1c\\int_\\Sigma H_{x\\to z}\\!\\left(-T^\\sharp(n_x)\\right)d\\Sigma,\\qquad S_z:=\\frac1c\\int_\\Sigma v(x)\\wedge H_{x\\to z}\\!\\left(-T^\\sharp(n_x)\\right)d\\Sigma,$$\nwhere $(v\\wedge w)^{ab}:=v^aw^b-v^bw^a$ and the finite-dimensional vector and tensor integrals are taken componentwise in any basis of $T_zM$. The resulting $p_z$ and $S_z$ are a vector and an antisymmetric tensor at $z$; their units are $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$ and $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-1}}$.",
        "strategy": "not-applicable",
