        "statement": "Adopt the charged-body quantities and SI tensor convention of [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]] and [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], the polarization transport postulate [[post-rpm-bmt-dipole-model]], and its translational equation obtained from [[pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence]]. On a local potential domain, assume the finite curve segments are stationary for the adopted action. Let $z$ be a $C^2$ future timelike proper-time curve, $U=dz/d\\tau$, $g(U,U)=-c^2$, with prescribed continuous alternating field $F$ along it. Let $m>0$, $q\\in\\mathbb R$, $g_s\\in\\mathbb R$, and an initial $C^1$ spin vector satisfy $g(s,U)=0$. Put $\\alpha=q/m$, $G=g_s/2$, and $\\kappa=g(s,F^\\sharp(U))$. The adopted equations and action result give\n\n$$D_\\tau U=\\alpha F^\\sharp(U),\\qquad D_\\tau s=\\alpha\\left[G F^\\sharp(s)+(1-G)\\frac{U}{c^2}\\kappa\\right].$$\n\nThen $g(s,U)=0$ and $g(s,s)$ are constant along every solution. In the restricted ansatz $D_\\tau s=\\alpha[aF^\\sharp(s)+bU\\kappa/c^2]$, preservation for arbitrary initial data requires $a+b=1$ when $\\alpha\\ne0$; matching the specified rest-frame law $d\\mathbf s/d\\tau=\\alpha G(\\mathbf s\\times\\mathbf B)$ with $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$ then gives $a=G$, $b=1-G$. This coefficient conclusion is only within that ansatz. If $\\alpha=0$, the rest-frame matching condition does not identify $a,b$.",
