        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-rpm-charge-spin-model": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence": "physical-result",
          "post-rpm-bmt-dipole-model": "physical-assumption",
          "pthm-rpm-bmt-constraints": "physical-result"
        },
        "statement": "Use the charged-body conventions and adopted BMT transport equation of [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]] and [[post-rpm-bmt-dipole-model]]. On a local potential domain, take the trajectory stationary under the action postulate so that [[pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence]] supplies its translation equation. Let $U$ be future timelike, $g(U,U)=-c^2$, and $g(U,s)=0$. Write $\\alpha=q/m$, $G=g_s/2$, $\\kappa=g(s,F^\\sharp(U))$, and $a=D_\\tau U=\\alpha F^\\sharp(U)$. The BMT law is\n$$D_\\tau s=\\alpha\\left[GF^\\sharp(s)-(G-1)\\frac{U\\kappa}{c^2}\\right].$$\n\n(a) If $g_s=0$ ($G=0$), then $D_\\tau s=Ug(a,s)/c^2$, the Fermi–Walker transport law on $U^\\perp$.\n\n(b) If $g_s=2$ ($G=1$), then $D_\\tau s=\\alpha F^\\sharp(s)$ while $D_\\tau U=\\alpha F^\\sharp(U)$, so the same linear field operator transports both vectors. Both parameter cases preserve orthogonality and spin norm by [[pthm-rpm-bmt-constraints]].",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": [
          {
            "title": "University of Virginia, Relativistic Spin Precession: the BMT Equation, Electromagnetic Theory lecture",
