        "physical_scope": "Exact parameter specializations of the adopted classical BMT transport model: $g_s=0$ gives Fermi–Walker transport on the orthogonal spin space, and $g_s=2$ makes the same field generator act on $U$ and $s$. These are model deductions, not measured cases.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-rpm-fixed-versus-scaled-charge",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "physics",
        "library": "physics",
        "title": "Fixed-charge and scaled small-body limits",
        "reference": "items/ex-rpm-fixed-versus-scaled-charge.md#Example",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored in assigned batch; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "deps": [
          "pthm-rpm-spherical-charge-size-bound"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "pthm-rpm-spherical-charge-size-bound": "physical-result"
        },
        "statement": "Assume the static spherical-body hypotheses of [[pthm-rpm-spherical-charge-size-bound]], so every member obeys $\\ell\\ge q^2/(8\\pi\\varepsilon_0Mc^2)$.\n\n(a) For fixed $q\\ne0$ and fixed finite $M>0$, the lower bound is the positive constant $q^2/(8\\pi\\varepsilon_0Mc^2)$. Thus no family satisfying these hypotheses can have $\\ell\\to0$ while keeping both $q$ and $M$ fixed.\n\n(b) For $q(\\lambda)=\\lambda q_0$, $M(\\lambda)=\\lambda M_0$, and $\\ell(\\lambda)=\\lambda\\ell_0$ with $\\lambda>0$, $q_0\\ne0$ coulombs, $M_0>0$ kilograms, and $\\ell_0>0$ metres, the same necessary condition reduces to $\\ell_0\\ge q_0^2/(8\\pi\\varepsilon_0M_0c^2)$, independent of $\\lambda$. The inequality therefore does not rule out this scaling, although it does not construct a body family or prove that its interior energy remains admissible.",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": [
          {
            "title": "Konstantin K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Classical Electrodynamics, §1.3 Scalar Potential and Electric Field Energy",
